WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.850
So we have some big news from the Corona virus
testing vaccine front.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.410
There are a ton of Corona virus vaccines in
development.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:10.710
We've talked about some of them.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:15.250
Some are sort of closer to a finished vaccine
than others.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:20.810
And one of the prominent vaccines is the Oxford
Kovik 19 vaccine.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:25.710
We talked last week, maybe it was the week
before about the Madona vaccine.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:29.350
Another one of the more advanced vaccines
is the Oxford vaccine.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:35.519
And this month, they are going to be moving
into phase two and three trials, which will

00:00:35.519 --> 00:00:38.820
encompass a total of about 40000 people.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:46.519
So 40000 people, 40000 volunteers are about
to receive the Oxford Corona virus vaccine,

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:48.000
which is huge.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.730
It's going to provide very important information
about the effectiveness of the vaccine at

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.489
preventing corona virus.

00:00:55.489 --> 00:01:00.690
So just to remind you sort of the process,
safety trials were done before phase one.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:05.050
The question is, does the human body tolerate
this?

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:10.220
Aside from whether it will make you immune,
does the body tolerate this safely?

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.070
Are there side effects, et cetera?

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:15.110
We got past that with the Oxford vaccine.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:16.300
Then we had a phase one trial.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.670
Phase one trial is a relatively small group.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:19.900
It was done in April.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.720
1000 people were immunized.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.710
A follow up is happening now.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:33.940
So far, phase one has determined that the
vaccine is causing the body to generate antibodies.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.090
That's very good.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:42.210
So this next part is going to add about 10000
people in a phase to trial, which will expand

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:48.360
the ages that those first thousand were all
adults, but not older adults.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.730
So we're going to fight.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:54.910
We're going to now in phase to see 10000 people,
which will include some older adults and some

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:57.570
kids receive the vaccine.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:05.490
The point of phase two is, does the age of
the person affect antibody generation?

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:10.289
Do older adults generate the same antibodies
as younger adults?

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:15.079
Do children generate the same antibodies with
this vaccine as younger adults?

00:02:15.079 --> 00:02:19.660
Then there will, I believe, simultaneously
be a phase three trial going on, which is

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:24.629
going to, again, just focus on a narrower
age range of adults.

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:30.430
But it will be a test for efficacy with an
additional 30000 individuals at least.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:35.290
And then the question is, again, we're going
to go from a thousand people to 30000 and

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:40.680
see how many of them are generating antibodies
after receiving this vaccine.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.000
That phase three will also include a control
group, which is a very important thing.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.659
So the phase two three trials combined will
include over 40000 people.

00:02:50.659 --> 00:02:53.209
We're going to get very valuable data from
this.

00:02:53.209 --> 00:02:56.209
And it does take some time with the early
trials.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:03:03.209
We've seen that it can take upwards of 40
days for the body to get to its full antibody

00:03:03.209 --> 00:03:05.900
generation after receiving the vaccination.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:11.829
So once you have the antibodies, which can
take 40 days, 43 days in one study, then you

00:03:11.829 --> 00:03:13.549
need to follow all of those people and see.

00:03:13.549 --> 00:03:15.029
We'll do do they get coronavirus?

00:03:15.029 --> 00:03:18.760
Does this actually work to protect them from
corona virus?

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.170
And of course, how long does it provide immunity?

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.200
You could you could say after three months.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.540
Well, it seems like this vaccine is providing
at least three months of immunity.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.379
But you need six months of following those
people to be able to say, well, it looks like

00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:37.700
it provides six months of immunity.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.560
And that's something that if there's an early
enough efficacy signal.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:49.120
So imagine that by October, they say, well,
it seems to provide at least five months of

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.120
immunity.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:54.219
You could potentially get an emergency use
authorization and say, hey, if it provides

00:03:54.219 --> 00:03:57.650
five months of immunity and it's safe, let's
start giving it to health care workers.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:03:59.400
Five months is better than nothing.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.599
Now, could this be sped up at all?

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:04.549
I've mentioned challenge trials in the past.

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:08.950
Typically, challenge trials are not done with
a vaccine like this.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:15.889
A challenge trial would be rather than doing
phase three, where you just follow people

00:04:15.889 --> 00:04:21.539
and see if they get infected at a lower rate
than those in the general population.

00:04:21.539 --> 00:04:24.430
You can deliberately expose people to the
virus.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.930
That's what a challenge trial would be called.

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:32.479
That could potentially shorten phase three
and more quickly demonstrate that it generates

00:04:32.479 --> 00:04:35.349
antibodies that provide protection.

00:04:35.349 --> 00:04:37.889
It wouldn't tell you how long they last.

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:38.889
Maybe they last a year.

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:43.340
But you need a year to go by to know that
there is a debate about whether a challenge

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:46.330
trials should be done now outside of this
vaccine.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:50.361
If you were interested in participating in
a challenge trial around Corona virus, there

00:04:50.361 --> 00:04:52.479
are lots of different kinds going on.

00:04:52.479 --> 00:04:54.810
Not necessarily for this vaccine.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.740
You can find out about that at one day sooner,
dawg.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:58.919
That's the number one.

00:04:58.919 --> 00:05:01.240
A sooner or one day sooner.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.240
Dot org.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.500
The other question, when we're developing
a new vaccine and trying to figure out if

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:08.530
it works is manufacturing capacity.

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:10.379
So I've talked about this before.

00:05:10.379 --> 00:05:17.370
If you wait to start manufacturing huge amounts
of the vaccine until you have results from

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:23.020
a Phase two three trial, it's too late in
the sense that it will take months with the

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.650
flu vaccine that starts to be available.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:29.490
Don't hold me to this, but I think the flu
vaccine in in the U.S. starts to be available

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.099
like August, September.

00:05:31.099 --> 00:05:33.360
Typically, they start manufacturing that.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.599
I believe it's April, roughly six months before.

00:05:36.599 --> 00:05:37.770
Five months before.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:42.259
So what is happening here is that Oxford is
partnering with AstraZeneca.

00:05:42.259 --> 00:05:47.670
They are now going to start manufacturing
large doses of this vaccine so that if it

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:53.199
does work well, there will potentially be,
you know, a hundred million doses total by

00:05:53.199 --> 00:05:55.889
the end of the year from that batch.

00:05:55.889 --> 00:05:58.699
Thirty million would be for the UK.

00:05:58.699 --> 00:06:02.500
This is the Oxford vaccine and 80 million
would be for the rest of the world.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:05.699
So cautious optimism is the right approach
right now.

00:06:05.699 --> 00:06:08.009
This is one of the many vaccines that we've
talked about.

00:06:08.009 --> 00:06:09.009
Last week.

00:06:09.009 --> 00:06:10.009
We talked about Maduna.

00:06:10.009 --> 00:06:15.849
We hope to have at least a couple of these
both work and be manufactured in bulk in advance

00:06:15.849 --> 00:06:20.990
so that by the end of the year we could at
least hopefully dose several million Americans

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:22.080
with it.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.840
And if you sit, I don't know what's going
to happen.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:29.650
Of course, if you said, David, you've got
a bet, what would you bet is going to happen

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:31.080
with the vaccine?

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:37.319
My bet would be by the end of the year, there
will be millions of Americans vaccinated.

00:06:37.319 --> 00:06:40.220
Now we're a country of 330 million people.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:45.710
So if three million people are vaccinated
by December 31, it's both no huge win and

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:47.970
it's also not that unlikely.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.050
But that's where I would put my money right
now.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:55.789
I believe the U.S. will be in the millions
of vaccinations by the end of the year, but

00:06:55.789 --> 00:07:01.229
it's going to take longer to really start
to approach what could even remotely be referred

00:07:01.229 --> 00:07:06.930
to as herd immunity, which for something at
the contagion level of corona virus, is estimated

00:07:06.930 --> 00:08:20.690
at about 70 percent immunity.

